# broiler-js retained workload pin 1
#
# A pin this repository holds for a workload it now also holds. `revision` is the immutable
# upstream commit - never a branch name - and `content-sha256` is the digest over every path and
# content the checkout of that revision produces.
#
# WHY THE AUTHORITY IS HERE AND NOT IN THE CHECKOUT. The workload roadmap's JSW-10 asks that the
# Octane checkout be "retrieved, hashed and archived so a run against it means something the next
# reader can check", the way the conformance suite's pin already works. Until this file existed the
# end-user host took a path and kept no copy, so no digest stood behind any benchmark answer and
# pointing a tool at a directory performed none of the retrieve-hash-archive the ledger records as
# the human action.
#
# HOW IT WAS TAKEN. Cloned from the upstream repository on 2026-09-04 and archived by `git archive`
# at the named commit, so the bytes are a function of the commit rather than of the filesystem that
# held it: no mtimes, no ordering that depends on a directory read, and `gzip -n` so the archive
# carries no timestamp of its own. `content-sha256` was then computed over the archive's own
# members, which is the digest a reader can recompute without a network.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOT. It is not a benchmark result and it authorises none. Octane is RETIRED upstream -
# the pinned commit is the retirement commit - and a score from it is a number about this
# configuration rather than a comparison with anything. Roadmap section 17's measurement discipline
# is JS-10's and nothing here anticipates it.
#
# It is separately licensed; the upstream terms are retained beside this file.

suite octane
